That's only true for the simple test case.

The following code causes the same error and cannot be modified as proposed:

```
function foo(number) {
    var string = (number + 1) + 'px';
    window.alert(string);
}
```

@Steffen185 you're totally right. I outlined the issue in this code:

``` js
const number = 10;

// we want to do this because of type evaluation order
console.log(number + 1 + 'px'); // => 11px

// what no-useless-concat thinks we want to do; but not really
console.log(number + '1px'); // => 101px

// note the early string type coercion
console.log('' + number + 1 + 'px'); // => 101px
```

---

@hzoo Type coercion order must be considered. See above.

@hzoo I think it can be simpler than trying all the types. 

Suppose new rule implementation is called `no-useless-concat_new`. And let current implementation be called `no-useless-concat_old`.
For string concatenation, `A_0 + A_1 + ... + A_n`, if you can find `A_j` that is a literal string (`0 <= j < n`), and subexpression `A_(j+1) + ... + A_n` satisfies `no-useless-concat_old` rule, then `no-useless-concat_new` is satisfied.

issue.body:
`prefer-template` doesn't consider unary expressions in string concat as warning. 

For example:

``` js
// EDIT: wrong one
//var foo = 'bar' + (typeof bar) + 'baz';

var foo = 'bar' + (typeof 100) + 'baz';
```

It can be rewritten as the following; but `prefer-template` doesn't emit a warning:

``` js
var foo = `bar ${typeof 100} baz`;
```

